<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sendai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sendai is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyagi-prefecture">Miyagi Prefecture</a> and the largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku region</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It was laid out from 1601 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> around his castle and was the seat of the Sendai Domain throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. Today it has about a million inhabitants and is known as the City of Trees.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sendai.jpg" alt="Sendai" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nryate · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SendaiCity_Skylines_from_Mukaiyama2018.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sendai stretches from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> to the Ōu Mountains, which form the east and west borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyagi-prefecture">Miyagi Prefecture</a>. Its eastern part is a plain, its centre hilly and its west mountainous, and the highest point is Mount Funagata at 1,500 metres. Unusually for a large Japanese coastal city, the city core stands on a terrace 40 to 60 metres above sea level. The area was inhabited as early as 20,000 years ago, though the history of Sendai as a city begins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a>. It became a designated city in 1989 and has five wards, whose names deliberately avoid directions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> was unhappy with his previous stronghold at Iwadeyama, in the north of his territories and hard to reach from Edo. Sendai lay in the centre of his newly defined lands on the major road from Edo, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> gave him permission to build a new castle on Aobayama after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a>. The name had been written with characters meaning a thousand generations, and Masamune changed it to characters meaning a hermit and a platform. The characters come from a Chinese poem praising a palace of the Han emperor Wen, and tradition says Masamune chose them so that the castle would prosper like a mountain inhabited by an immortal hermit.</p><p>In 1613 Masamune built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleon</a> to send an embassy headed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasekura-tsunenaga">Hasekura Tsunenaga</a> to the Pope in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The embassy established relations with the Holy See, but the suppression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> frustrated Masamune's plans. The first railway between Sendai and Tokyo, now the Tōhoku Main Line, opened in 1887 and put the area within a day's travel of Tokyo. Tohoku Imperial University was founded in 1907 and became the first Japanese university to admit women in 1913. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the city was rebuilt as a transport and logistics hub for the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sendai is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyagi-prefecture">Miyagi Prefecture</a> and the largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku region</a>. It is known as the City of Trees, because of the zelkova trees along Jōzenji Street and Aoba Street, and its Tanabata Festival is the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai-castle">Sendai Castle</a> stand on Aobayama, and the Zuihōden is the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a>. The Ōsaki Hachimangū shaden, built by Masamune in 1607, is a National Treasure. On 11 March 2011 coastal areas of the city suffered severe damage from a magnitude 9.0 offshore earthquake and the tsunami it caused.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sendai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An earthquake of an estimated magnitude of at least 8.6, possibly 9.0, struck around <b>Sendai</b> in northern Honshu, and its tsunami caused widespread flooding of the Sendai plain.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-9">9 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sendai</b> and Yonezawa sought pardon for them, and when this was refused, delegates met at Shiroishi in May 1868 and formed an alliance, joined by Nagaoka and other Echigo domains.</blockquote><a href="/state/ouetsu-reppan-domei">Ouetsu Reppan Domei</a></li><li><blockquote>Date Masamune, who had sided with Tokugawa Ieyasu in 1600, began building Aoba castle at <b>Sendai</b> in 1601 and laid out the city around it.</blockquote><a href="/state/sendai-domain">Sendai Domain</a></li></ul></section>
